Add a clock, a greeting and a quote of the day to the new tab page.

The three things a Tabliss-style new tab page offers that the speed dial and
the background chooser next to them do not, held to the standard those two
already set: nothing here is ever fetched. A live weather widget or a fetched
background needs a request this page has no business making by default - the
quote list below is compiled into the script instead, twenty-four lines of
text long enough out of copyright to need no licence, and the clock and
greeting read the browser's own clock. Same list as AeriumNtpWidgets.java on
the Android build, for the same reason it gives: a few quotes commonly found
to be misattributed (an "Aristotle" line on habit that is really Will
Durant's summary of him, an "Emerson" line absent from his actual writing, a
"Leonardo" line traced to a 1931 book about him) were left out rather than
repeated.

Rides in the same settings JSON blob as the background and layout choices -
cfg.clock, cfg.greeting, cfg.quote, cfg.name - for the reason the patch that
introduced that blob already gives: the page is the only reader and writer,
so a pref per checkbox would be four more registrations to say the same
thing an object key does. All three widgets default on; the name is empty by
default, and the greeting reads "Good morning" and similar without one.

Refreshed every 30 seconds while the page is open, the same interval and the
same reasoning as AeriumNtpWidgets' Handler on Android: readable, not exact
to the second, and cheap enough not to matter on a page people glance at and
leave.

Verified the same way aerium-newtab-page.patch's own settings addition was:
built against the tree that patch produces, the script re-extracted and run
through node --check, every new getElementById target cross-checked against
the ids added to the markup in this same patch, and the raw string rescanned
for a premature terminator.

#widgets hides as a whole when every row inside it is off, not only its
children individually - an oversight in the first version of this patch,
caught before either desktop repo had shipped a release with it. Without
that, turning off the clock, the greeting and the quote all at once left the
container's own margin behind: an empty 40px gap sitting above the shortcuts
for nothing. The dead `#widgets:empty` rule this was meant to lean on is
removed with it - `:empty` never matches here, since the five child divs stay
in the DOM and are only ever individually hidden.

TWO FOLLOW-UPS, both requested after the fact and both mirrored on the
Android build's AeriumNtpWidgets.java so the two pages behave the same way.

cfg.quoteOffset. Read alone, dailyQuoteIndex() is a pure function of the
calendar date, which means every install of this browser would open on the
exact same quote on the exact same day - fine for one person, odd once you
picture the whole install base synchronised. Generated once, the first time
settings are ever read back (a fresh profile and one upgrading from before
this field existed both read as "not a number" here), folded into the index,
and saved immediately so it survives a reload.

Tap to reshuffle. #wQuote and #wAuthor are clickable; either one advances to
the next entry in QUOTES and re-renders just those two lines, with a quick
opacity dip so the change reads as a response to the click. Only a reload
forgets it - quoteIndex starts null, and window.aeriumNewTabSettings is the
only place that ever sets it from the day/offset formula, once, when real
settings first arrive.

That was not the first shape this took. The first version had renderWidgets()
recompute quoteIndex from the formula on every call, which sounds like "once
per visit" but is not: applyCfg() (and so renderWidgets()) runs on every
settings-panel click, including background, layout and photo changes that
have nothing to do with the quote. A reshuffle would survive the 30-second
interval and then get undone by clicking "3 per row". Fixed by moving the
formula out of renderWidgets() entirely and into the settings handler, and
splitting renderWidgets() into updateClockAndGreeting() (what the interval
calls) and renderQuote() (what a reshuffle calls and what renderWidgets()
now only re-renders from, never recomputes).

Caught before either desktop repo had shipped a release with the reshuffle at
all, the same way the two bugs above it were: rebuilding the tree this patch
produces and working the settings panel by hand rather than trusting the
first draft.

Re-verified the same way as the rest of this patch: node --check on the
re-extracted script, every id cross-checked, the raw string rescanned for a
premature terminator - which caught a second, unrelated mistake this time: a
code comment reading `dailyQuoteIndex()"` contains the same )" sequence the
placeholder fix earlier in this file was about, and would have closed the
raw string early. Reworded rather than repeated.

